llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232): give gfx803 its
own MMQ tile table, without the tile widths that spill registers on it.

WHY
---
On gfx803, prompt processing with a K-quant model runs at about a tenth of
the speed the matmul kernels can reach, and slower than the CPU. Qwen2.5-Coder
7B Q4_K_M on an RX 590: llama-bench pp512 15.2 tok/s (a Ryzen 7 2700X does
about 26), while token generation is a normal 30 tok/s.

Almost all of that time is the quantized matmul (MMQ) at large batch sizes.
test-backend-ops perf, MUL_MAT q4_K x f32, m=4096 k=14336: 1.06 TFLOPS at
n=8 but 0.17 TFLOPS at n=512. For a large batch MMQ picks the widest tile
the GCN table offers (J=64 columns, I=128 rows), and on gfx803 that kernel
spills 565 VGPRs to scratch memory.

The GCN table is shared by gfx803 (GCN4) and gfx900/gfx906 (GCN5), and its
occupancy of 2 caps every MMQ kernel at 128 VGPRs. gfx906 does each int8 dot
product with one v_dot4 instruction. gfx803 has no dot instruction, so
ggml_cuda_dp4a falls back to byte extracts and 24-bit multiply-adds, which
hold more values in registers. The wide tiles no longer fit in 128 VGPRs and
spill, and the slowdown follows the spill count. Q4_K, batch 512, TFLOPS by
largest J allowed (VGPRs spilled at that J):

    J=8  1.76 (0)    J=16 2.41 (36)   J=24 1.99 (0)    J=32 1.55 (94)
    J=40 0.76 (167)  J=48 0.54 (268)  J=64 0.17 (565)

The same sweep for the other K-quants (TFLOPS, default = J up to 128):

    Q2_K  default 0.33, best 1.65 at J=8
    Q4_K  default 0.17, best 2.41 at J=16
    Q5_K  default 1.87, best 2.03 at J=32 (and 0.73 at J=40)
    Q6_K  default 1.84, best 2.18 at J=32
    Q3_K  default 2.03, best 2.06 at J=48: unchanged

Q8_0, Q4_0 and IQ4_XS already run fastest with the default tiles (2.78, 2.66
and 2.44 TFLOPS), so they are unchanged. Running the wide tiles at occupancy 1
(256 VGPRs) instead removes the cliff but peaks lower (Q4_K 2.11 TFLOPS at
J=24), so the table keeps occupancy 2 and drops the slow widths.

WHAT
----
mmq-config-gcn4.cuh is the GCN table with the Q2_K tiles wider than 8, the
Q4_K tiles wider than 16, and the Q5_K and Q6_K tiles wider than 32 removed.
mmq.cuh selects it for gfx803 on the host (cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4) and in
device code (GCN4, defined for __gfx803__ in vendors/hip.h). The J selection
loop skips widths a table does not define, so it stops at the widest
remaining one. gfx900 and gfx906 still use the GCN table.

Result, same model and card, llama-bench, flash attention on: pp512 15.2 ->
156.7 tok/s, tg64 29.9 -> 29.9 tok/s; pp2048 with ubatch 1024 runs at 129.7.

Verified on an RX 590: test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 passes MUL_MAT
1301/1301 and MUL_MAT_ID 931/931 against the CPU backend, the same as
without the patch. Through Ollama built with Dockerfile-Ollama, a
1759-token prompt goes from 14.8 to 129.8 tok/s and greedy output is
token-for-token identical to the unpatched build. To reproduce, build llama.cpp b11232 with GGML_HIP=ON and
AMDGPU_TARGETS=gfx803, then run, with and without the patch:

    test-backend-ops perf -b ROCm0 -o MUL_MAT -p "type_a=q4_K,type_b=f32,m=4096,n=512"
    llama-bench -m <a Q4_K_M model> -ngl 99 -p 512 -n 0

Ollama applies every *.patch under llama/compat to the fetched llama.cpp in
filename order; Dockerfile-Ollama copies this one there and checks that it
was applied.

